# What never reaches the build context.
#
# The first entry is the one that matters. backend/config/ holds the real mailbox
# app password and the key that decrypts every stored mailbox password. It is
# excluded from git for that reason, and a Docker build would otherwise copy it
# into an image layer — where it stays readable forever, even if a later layer
# deletes it, and travels with the image to wherever it is pushed.
backend/config/

# Environment files. Compose reads these on the host and passes the values in as
# variables; nothing inside the image ever opens them.
.env
*.env
.env.example

# Build output. The image builds its own; carrying a stale copy in would only
# invalidate cache layers and risk shipping an interface older than the code.
backend/target/
backend/data/
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/dist/
backend/src/main/resources/static/

# History and tooling
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml

# Documentation and scripts, none of which the application reads at runtime
*.md
LICENSE
go-online.ps1
docker-compose.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# Noise
*.log
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
